.TH SMTFEAT 1
.SH NAME
smtfeat \- structural features of SMT formulas
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smtfeat
[
.B \-u
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I smtfeat
reads SMT-LIB formulas and writes, one per formula, the line of a table of
structural features: all that a solver sees of a formula before it starts to work.
The first line of the output names the columns, and a tab separates one field from the next.
.PP
The input is a formula in the SMT-LIB language, a
.IR tar (1)
archive of formulas, or either compressed with
.IR zstd :
the first bytes tell them apart.
An archive holds the benchmarks as
.IR non-incremental/ logic / family / file ,
and names each one
.IR logic / family / file .
An argument of
.B \-
reads the standard input, as does no argument at all.
.PP
Every operator is counted once an application:
.B (bvadd x y)
holds one
.BR bvadd ,
and
.B ((_ extract 7 0) x)
one
.BR extract .
The head of a term that the vocabulary lacks, as the macro of a benchmark called in a term,
counts under
.B other
and is listed by
.BR \-u .
.PP
The columns before the operators:
.TP
.B id, logic, status
The name of the benchmark, the logic it declares, and the answer it declares: sat, unsat or unknown.
.TP
.B bytes
The size of the formula in bytes.
.TP
.B asserts, checks
The assertions, and the
.B check-sat
commands.
.TP
.B decls, defs
The symbols declared, and the commands that define a function.
.TP
.B depth
The deepest nesting of lists in an asserted term or in the body of a definition:
the number the SMT-LIB catalog gives.
.TP
.B nops, mulrat
The applications of every operator, and the share of the multiplicative and division ones among them.
.TP
.B bits, wmax, wdist
The bitvector widths of the declared variables: their sum, their largest, and how many differ.
.TP
.B arrays
The declared variables of array sort.
.TP
.B bvlits
The bitvector literals.
.TP
.B other
The applications of symbols the vocabulary lacks.
.PP
Then a count a column for every operator and binder: the core ones as
.B =
and
.BR let ,
the bitvector ones as
.B bvmul
and
.BR extract ,
and
.B select
and
.B store
of arrays.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
The features of a whole archive:
.RS
.nf
smtfeat QF_BV.tar.zst > QF_BV.tsv
.fi
.RE
.PP
The heads the vocabulary lacks, each with its count, for
.IR sort (1)
and
.IR awk (1)
to sum:
.RS
.nf
smtfeat \-u QF_ABV.tar.zst
.fi
.RE
.PP
One formula off the hand, through the standard input:
.RS
.nf
echo \(aa(assert (bvadd x y))\(aa | smtfeat
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A stream that is neither a formula nor an archive yields a table of zeros, not a complaint:
the scanner reads tokens, and does not judge them.
.SH SOURCE
The archives come from the SMT-LIB release on Zenodo.
The vocabulary covers the core, bitvector and array theories of SMT-LIB;
one for other theories is a longer table in
.IR src/feat.rs .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR smtdata (1),
.IR smtstat (1),
.IR smtfit (1)
